	In other words, $l^{*}$ is $l^{\bar u}$ except in the circumstance that $u$ is true and some outer clause is falsified, and in that case and only that case we have $l^{*}$ takes the value from $l^{ u}$.
	The definition is consistent with negation (i.e. $\neg l^{*}= (\neg l)^*$). The dependency set of $l^*$ is no greater than that of $l$.

	One may notice that if there were Skolem functions that satisfied $\forall U \exists E \psi$, these Skolem functions extended to the new $*$-variables would also satisfy $\chi_0^{*}$, which replaces all $x$ variables with $x^{*}$ in $\chi_0$. This is because if $u$ is false every $l^{*}$ plays as $l^{\bar u}$ which is how $l$ plays anyway. 
