\begin{abstract}
    Randomized methods such as \PRM and \RRT are widely used in motion
    planning. However, in some cases, their running-time suffers from
    inherent instability, leading to ``catastrophic'' performance even
    for relatively simple instances.  We apply stochastic
    restart %
    techniques, some of them new, for speeding up Las Vegas
    algorithms, that provide dramatic speedups in practice (a factor
    of $3$ [or larger] in many cases).

