    flatmap class transformation:
    \begin{itemize}[itemsep=0pt, topsep=0pt, left=0pt]
        \item Case 1: This case involves transformations where the value in the key-value pair is split into several components, each paired with the original key to form multiple new key-value pairs. An example of this transformation is converting row-wise data into columnar data. In that scenario, the value (v), composed of multiple columns, is divided into smaller groups of columns (vi), each of which remains associated with the original key. These new key-value pairs are stored in a different structure Ti, separate from the original structure T. 

        \item Case 2: This case represents a scenario where both the key and the value in the input pair are transformed, resulting in multiple new key-value pairs. The original key and the value are no longer present after the transformation. A common example of this type of transformation is data normalization, where the original key-value pair set in the original structure T gets normalized into n sets of key-value pairs, which are then stored in n distinct structures Ti.

        \item Case 3: This case represents transformations that preserve the original key-value pair while also generating one or more additional key-value pairs, where the value is the original key. A typical example of this transformation is the creation of secondary indexes, where new key-value pairs are produced to facilitate faster lookups, with the value of the new pair being the original key.

        \item Case 4: This case represents scenarios where the original key-value pair is preserved, but one or more additional key-value pairs are generated, with both the keys and values differing from the original pair. A common example of this transformation is the creation of a projected materialized view. In this scenario, the original data is retained and stored in the original structure T, while the materialized views, comprising new key-value pairs, are stored in separate structures Ti, where i ranges from 1 to n.
    \end{itemize}
