\subsection{Time complexity analysis}
When discussing the performance of \ac{DNN}, particularly in real-time applications, two key metrics are often used: \textit{latency} and \textit{throughput}. These metrics are critical for evaluating the efficiency and responsiveness of models, especially when deploying them on edge devices or in systems where timing is crucial~\cite{Hanhirova_2018_ACMMSC}.

\textit{Latency} in this context refers to the time required for a single input to pass through a \ac{DNN}, returning with some result. This is normally measured in milliseconds. Considering our scenario, it is important to have low latency so that decisions can be fast. For example, a high latency in the object detection model may result in a hazardous delay in the reaction to an obstacle by an autonomous car~\cite{Hanhirova_2018_ACMMSC}. Among these, latency depends on the input size, model architecture—depth, width, or the number of parameters—and hardware characteristics: CPU, GPU, or custom accelerators like TPUs. \textit{Throughput}, on the other hand, refers to the overall quantity of inputs that a network could process within a certain time frame, usually measured in frames per second or inputs per second. Throughput will apply in scenarios where large amounts of data need to be handled in less time; for instance, video, which requires many frames to be processed within a very short time. High throughput is quite critical in order to manage such volumes efficiently. Some of the key factors that influence throughput include how well the model can handle batch processing and how well it is parallelized on hardware. Unlike latency, it is throughput that determines the overall capacity of the system in view of continuous data processing like \ac{TD}.

These two metrics are interrelated but often involve trade-offs. Increased throughput by processing larger batches of data can sometimes increase latency, as the model waits for enough data to form a batch before it runs. Optimizations to reduce latency, such as processing smaller batches or reducing model complexity, may have disadvantages with respect to throughput.

For correctly measuring the time per inference from the view of an end-user, we exclude any initialization time, like library loading, live input setup, model weight loading, because these are one-time costs when a device is setting up~\cite{Hadidi_2019_IISWC}.

In Figure~\ref{fig:inference_time} we show the inference time normalized for the number of samples with different compressors, detectors, and batch size. The inference time is the time needed to process a batch of point clouds with the object detector, and it is normalized for the number of samples in the batch. 

We can see that the inference time for PointPillars and SECOND does not depend on the compression used. This is due to the fact that the object detectors process the point clouds in a structured format like a pseudo-image or a voxel representation, thus the time needed to process the point clouds is not affected by the number of points in the point cloud. While the inference time for PV-RCNN is affected by the compression used, G-PCC discards points if they are in the same position after the quantization, leading a significant reduction in the number of points processed by the detector. In particular, the inference time for PV-RCNN with G-PCC is lower than the inference time with Draco, and within G-PCC the inference time is lower for the most aggressive compression levels.

It is worth noting that the inference time is higher for PV-RCNN for any possible combination of compressor, as it is the most complex detector in terms of the number of parameters and the number of operations needed to process the point clouds.

On the other side, the inference time for SECOND is the lowest.

\begin{summary}
The noise introduced by the compression has a larger impact on the performance of the object detectors for the pedestrian class than for the car class.
Point clouds compressed with G-PCC have a slightly higher average \ac{AP} than the ones compressed with Draco.
Second and PV-RCNN are more robust to the noise introduced by the compression, and they achieve better \ac{AP}. The inference time for the object detectors is not affected by the compression used, except for PV-RCNN, which has a lower inference time with G-PCC than with Draco. But SECOND is the detector with the lowest inference time.
\end{summary}


