\title{Weight Generation for ESD Classifier}

\begin{document}

\section{Weight Generation}

Weights are selected to be within the range $[-1, 1]$ such that a positive weight value indicates an ESD-exposed state and a negative weight value indicates a non-ESD-exposed state. 
They must compensate for the fact that the training data will contain more logs from error operation than base operation.
Weights are assigned as follows:

Let $B$ and $E$ be the lists of all base and error states, respectively. 

Let the weight for base states $w_b = -|E|$ and for error states $w_e = |B|$.

For a unique state $i$, let $b_i$ be the number of times state $i$ is present in $B$, and $e_i$ be the number of times it is present in $E$. 

For each unique state $i$, the normalized weight is defined as:
\begin{displaymath}
w_i = \frac{w_b * b_i + w_e * e_i}{|w_b * b_i| + |w_e * e_i|}
\end{displaymath}

which can also be written as
\begin{displaymath}
	w_i = \frac{-|E| * b_i + |B| * e_i}{|E| * b_i + |B| * e_i}
\end{displaymath}

The weights can be used to classify a log as follows:

Let $S_L$ be the set of unique states in log $L$, and $s_i$ the number of times state $i$ appears in the log. 

\begin{displaymath}
	C_L = \sum_i^{S_L} \frac{w_i * s_i}{|L|}
\end{displaymath}

If $C_L$ is positive, we classify the log as having experienced ESD, and if it is negative, we classify it as having not experienced ESD. 

\section{Adding Delta}

The delta parameter was introduced to prevent states that are nearly equally present in base and error logs from having weights very close to 0. 
This would reduce ambiguity when classifying logs with lots of these ambiguous states. 

Weights were instead implemented as $w_b = -|E| - \delta$ and $w_e = |B| + \delta$. 

Thus the per-state weight can be written as
\begin{displaymath}
	w_i = \frac{-|E| * b_i - \delta * b_i + |B| * e_i + \delta * e_i}{|E| * b_i + \delta * b_i + |B| * e_i + \delta * e_i}
\end{displaymath}

The following graphs show performance on several different training datasets for different values of delta. 
The top graph shows a sorted plot of unique weights; the dotted line is a straight line from -1 to 1. 
The bottom graph plots accuracy (black line), deviation from the dotted straight line (red line), and the absolute value of the middle weight (blue line). 
Of most interest are probably the weight distribution (top graph) and the accuracy plot. 

\includegraphics[scale=0.5]{figure_1}
\includegraphics[scale=0.5]{figure_3}
\includegraphics[scale=0.5]{figure_7}

\section{Scaling Before Normalization}

A different approach to the delta parameter is to multiply instead of add. 
If we scale before normalizing the weights, $w_b = -|E| * \delta$ and $w_e = |B| * \delta$. 

Thus the per-state weight can be written as
\begin{displaymath}
	w_i = \frac{-|E| * b_i * \delta + |B| * e_i * \delta}{|E| * b_i * \delta + |B| * e_i * \delta}
\end{displaymath}

The following graph shows the performance of this method. 
Note that the weights all fall into several of the exact same distribution instead of constantly varying as in the added delta method. 

\includegraphics[scale=0.5]{scale-before}

\section{Scaling After Normalization}

We can also scale weights after normalization. 
The base and error state weights are as they were without delta: $w_b = -|E|$ and $w_e = |B|$. 

Per-state weights are normalized and then scaled:
\begin{displaymath}
	w_i = (\frac{-|E| * b_i * \delta + |B| * e_i * \delta}{|E| * b_i * \delta + |B| * e_i * \delta}) * \delta
\end{displaymath}

The following graph shows the performance of this method.
As we would expect, weights are no longer in the $[-1, 1]$ range.
The classifier does not see any performance change because the classifier only uses sign, not magnitude, to perform log classification.

\includegraphics[scale=0.5]{scale-after}

\section{Further Improvement}

We can use our knowledge of classifier accuracy to improve the weights we have for each state. 
Weights can be improved iteratively by running the classifier, then increasing the weights for correctly classified states and decreasing the weights for incorrectly classified states.
Some potential concerns:
\begin{itemize}
	\item Additional iterations may increase overfitting to the training data.
	\item We expect many non-ESD-exposed states to be present in ESD-exposed logs. Weight adjustment must insure that base states do not become more likely to be classified as error states. 
\end{itemize}

