Self-regulated learning (SRL) is crucial for college students navigating increased academic demands and independence. Insufficient SRL skills can lead to disorganized study habits, low motivation, and poor time management, undermining learners’ ability to thrive in challenging environments. Through a formative study involving 59 college students, we identified key challenges students face in developing SRL skills, including difficulties with goal-setting, time management, and reflective learning. To address these challenges, we introduce SRLAgent, an LLM-assisted system that fosters SRL skills through gamification and adaptive support from large language models (LLMs). Grounded in Zimmerman’s three-phase SRL framework, SRLAgent enables students to engage in goal-setting, strategy execution, and self-reflection within an interactive game-based environment. The system offers real-time feedback and scaffolding powered by LLMs to support students’ independent study efforts. We evaluated SRLAgent using a between-subjects design, comparing it to a baseline system (SRL without Agent features) and a traditional multimedia learning condition. Results showed significant improvements in SRL skills within the SRLAgent group (\textit{p} < .001, Cohen’s \textit{d} = 0.234), and higher engagement compared to the baselines. This work highlights the value of embedding SRL scaffolding and real-time AI support within gamified environments, offering design implications for educational technologies that aim to promote deeper learning and metacognitive skill development.


% Self-regulated learning (SRL) is crucial for college students grappling with increased independence and academic demands. Insufficient SRL skills can lead to disorganized study habits, low motivation, and poor time management, undermining learners’ ability to thrive in challenging environments. Through a formative study involving 59 college students, we identified key challenges and needs that college students face in developing self-regulated learning (SRL) skills. To address these findings, we introduce SRLAgent, an LLM-assisted system that fosters SRL skills through gamification and support from large language models (LLMs). Grounded in Zimmerman’s three-phase SRL framework, SRLAgent is designed to enhance students’ self-regulated learning (SRL) skills by enabling them to actively engage in and practice the entire SRL process. By LLMs, SRLAgent provides real-time guidance and problem-solving assistance throughout the process to scaffold students’ independent study efforts. We evaluated SRLAgent using a between-subjects design to examine its effectiveness on learners’ SRL skills and academic performance, comparing it against a baseline system and traditional learning method. Our findings indicate that SRLAgent improves students’ SRL outcomes and domain knowledge acquisition.{\textit{Study Findings}}{\textit{Discussions}}

% A two-stage formative study informed our system design. First, a questionnaire identified SRL deficits as a significant barrier in college students’ academic adjustment, leading to a focus on SRL skill development. Next, semi-structured interviews confirmed the demand for user-friendly mechanisms to support goal setting, strategic planning, and reflection. For evaluation, we propose a mixed-methods user study involving both within-subject and between-subject designs. Participants in the experimental condition will engage with SRLAgent’s complete functionality, while those in the control condition experience a standard Minecraft environment without SRL support. Pre- and post-intervention measurements via the Chinese version of the Online Self-Regulated Learning Questionnaire will quantify changes in SRL competencies. We anticipate that SRLAgent will significantly enhance college learners’ self-regulatory skills, offering a model for effective, scalable, and engaging educational technologies.

