Static strength is especially important for tasks requiring a locked wrist during full arm motion, such as lifting full grocery bags or a gallon of milk, each weighing roughly 4 kg. Including a maximum 1 kg end effector, a robotic wrist must sustain 5 kg of load in each axial direction.

Compliance integration may be accomplished by minimizing the torque needed to back-drive the actuators. At rest, human wrists require low forces to reorient the hand. For this, the robotic wrist must also allow the end effector to be reoriented with ease, warranting no more than 5 N of force. This translates to a back-driveability torque of at most 0.4 Nm. 
%when the actuator is not exerting torque.

\subsection{Speed, Bandwidth, Kinematics, and Precision}
To properly characterize wrist speed, past work designed a game where human subjects use isolated wrist motion to accomplish tasks \cite{vaisman_comparative_2013}. The study revealed peak wrist movement speeds were 10-53.3 RPM, providing a target range for robotic wrist speed quantities.

Studies examining human wrist responses to force perturbations and visual position targets provide useful information for characterizing bandwidth \cite{ito_visually-updated_nodate, forgaard_voluntary_2015}. Specifically, the long-latency reflex refers to a conscious wrist response to external stimuli. These findings indicate that the long-latency reflex occurs within a range of 50-100 ms, correlating to bandwidth frequencies of 10-20 Hz.

A study recorded human F/E and R/U angles using an electrogoniometer during ADL completion \cite{ryu_functional_1991}. For tasks involving personal hygiene and food preparation, it was found that 40\textdegree \space each of flexion and extension, 10\textdegree \space of radial deviation, and 30\textdegree \space of ulnar deviation is a reasonable representation of the range necessary for ADL completion as it was sufficient to complete 22 of the 24 tasks.

%Wrist angular precision has scarcely been evaluated directly. 
To get wrist angular precision, \cite{hong_evaluating_2016} and \cite{holman_accuracy_2020} both indicated minimum wrist angular precisions of 3.47\textdegree \space and 4.58\textdegree, respectively.

\subsection{Size and Weight}
The benefits of human wrist compactness are maintained in our size constraints defined by anthropometric data conducted by NASA \cite{noauthor_anthropometry_nodate}. The 95th percentile value of male human wrist measurements provides a wrist width and height of 61.4 mm. The actuators and power sources of our design are to be located within the forearm as it typically is unoccupied space in commercially available humanoid robots. Thus, the length of this robotic wrist is dictated by the maximum expected male forearm length of 349 mm \cite{noauthor_anthropometry_nodate}. To allow space for the elbow joint, wrist length is limited to 174.5 mm.

This robotic wrist would be attached to a robot arm in the same fashion any end effector would. We would like to model our desired weight using a typical example of a smaller gripper, such as the Robotiq gripper \cite{noauthor_2f-85_nodate}. This allows us to arrive to a final desired weight of approximately 1 kg. This is a standard weight that typical robot arms, such as the UR5e \cite{noauthor_ur5e_nodate}, can handle.

