\subsection{2-(R, RR) Parallel Kinematic Mechanism}
PKMs best emulate the F/E and R/U human wrist degrees of freedom and provide an efficient way of localizing degrees of freedom. This claim is directly investigated through the workspace comparison between the AgileX PiPER Arm with its serial wrist and the AgileX PiPER with a parallel wrist. One example of a task involving small workspace manipulation is reaching into a spice cabinet, where some kitchen configurations have deep angled cabinets. To represent this scenario in simulation, each arm was imported into the PyBullet environment along with a rendering of a deep angled cabinet. Inverse kinematics was performed for uniformly distributed target points across the inner cabinet workspace. If a joint pose exists for a tested target point without collisions with itself or the cabinet, the target point is considered reachable. This simulation found that the number of reachable points increased by 88\% when the parallel wrist is used instead of the serial wrist. Figure \ref{fig:kinematics} illustrates that the parallel wrist workspace encapsulates the serial wrist workspace and is also capable of reaching more surrounding points.

\begin{figure*}[t!]
    \centering\includegraphics[width=0.75\linewidth]{Figures/kinematics.png}
    \caption{\textbf{Left:} \textit{DexWrist} wrist simulation. \textbf{Middle:} Serial wrist simulation. \textbf{Right:} Workspace comparison plot illustrating reachability of each arm configuration within the deep angled cabinet. The \textit{DexWrist} improved constrained workspace reachability by 88\% when compared to the AgileX wrist.}
    \label{fig:kinematics}
\end{figure*}

%There are various ways to achieve the same workspace using parallel actuation: differential drives, ball joints, or other linkage configurations. Differential drives, used in \cite{damerla_design_2022}, are very space efficient and can exert high forces. However, since the degrees of freedom are not controlled independently, it lacks load transparency. Ball joints provide an alternative way of achieving a wide range of ROM, as exemplified in \cite{abe_abenics_2021, matilla_3_2023}. Actuating ball joints while reaching human wrist level torques proves to be difficult within a small form factor.

There are three main driving requirements in designing a decoupled 2-DOF PKM having high torque capabilities within human form factor: ROM, size, and load capacity. Figure \ref{fig:pkm} shows the 2-(R, RR) PKM designed for this robotic wrist, an adaptation of the 2-DOF Agile Eye \cite{gosselin_two_1999}, with a 1:1 transmission ratio. To ensure the linkage geometry has sufficient strength, four load cases were analyzed: 5 kg load in each axial direction (X,Y, and Z independently) and full 3 Nm exertion by both degrees of freedom. A minimum shaft diameter of 6mm is necessary for FOS = 3 and the smallest readily available off-the-shelf bushings have a 9 mm outer diameter and are 6 mm thick. Using these hardware specifications, linkage geometries were optimized to achieve the desired ROM without self-collisions and to fit within the prescribed size envelope while maintaining strength. The chosen linkage material is 17-4 PH Stainless Steel (\textit{$\sigma_{y}$} = 1345 MPa), resulting in a minimum FOS of 3.26 for the linkages under the four load cases.

