\subsection{Behavioral Cloning}


%===============================================================================

\subsubsection{Method}
To evaluate the impact of wrist design on manipulation performance, we trained diffusion policies \cite{chi2024diffusionpolicy} on 141 demonstrations recorded separately on the AgileX PiPER equipped with both the default wrist and the DexWrist (282 demonstrations total). For both system configurations, we train CNN-based diffusion policies  with identical hyperparameters, operating at 20Hz using a DDIM sampler \cite{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2010-02502} to perform the task. More details on the BC methodology and hyperparameters can be found in Appendix~\ref{app:diffusion_hyperparams}.


\subsubsection{Task Specification}
Retrieve an occluded flattened soda can from deep within a cluttered refrigerator and place it on the table. Failure occurs if any object is knocked over, the camera disconnects, or the refrigerator is displaced. Specifically, the task attempts to retrieve a flattened soda can which requires reaching deep into the back of the refrigerator, positioning the gripper such that the fingers are parallel to the back wall of the fridge (i.e., the gripper's opening axis is nearly orthogonal to the refrigerator's rear wall). This awkward orientation makes it difficult to avoid disturbing nearby objects during retrieval. Limited vertical clearance between shelves, dividing rack, and bottom tray creates minimal tolerance for arm movement. Initial object occlusion further increases task difficulty.
% The task is further constrained by the limited vertical clearance between shelves, as a dividing rack and bottom tray create minimal tolerance for arm movement without collisions or displacing the tray and its contents. Finally, the target object is highly occluded initially, making the task more difficult. 






\subsubsection{Evaluation} We evaluated the diffusion policies trained for each respective system at the same six epochs (75, 150, 225, 300, 375, 750). For each checkpoint, we collect 15 rollouts in the real environment (90 trials per system), randomly resetting the objects in the scene before each iteration. More details can be found in Table~\ref{tab:hyperparams} 

