\section{Discussion and Future Work}
\label{sec:discussion}
\vspace{-0.3em}
%The ability to teleoperate robots efficiently and to collect data in highly constrained environments resembling complex real-world environments is a significant step in scaling data collection and expanding the robot's capabilities. 
We empirically show that DexWrist reduces both the total time required (i.e., more intuitive teleoperation) and the average trajectory length of successful demonstrations provided by human teleoperators in constrained spaces. Reduction in the number of environment resets and total teleoperation time leads to a more efficient and therefore more scalable data collection process in constrained spaces, which is critical for scaling up data collection in consumer settings. The increased workspace of the robot with DexWrist enables completion of tasks that were not previously possible with the default wrist configuration in constrained environments. Lastly, torque transparency and backdrivability makes DexWrist capable of performing dynamic tasks via torque control. Videos of DexWrist performing highly dynamic tasks with human-level wrist dexterity can be found~\hyperlink{https://dexwrist.csail.mit.edu/}{here}.

 % The wrist demonstrated human-level dexterity in non-autonomous tasks, successfully performing both water bottle flips and pancake flipping. Videos of dynamic tasks can be found \hyperlink{ https://dexwrist.csail.mit.edu}{here}. 

