%an one-to-one correspondence between $D^1\bF$ and $I^1\bF$  (affine in $x\cdot \btheta$  with coefficients dependent on $(x \cdot \btheta^{\perp}, \btheta) $).
% Modulo this correspondence, the unique determination of the full vector field $\bF$ from $(I^0 f, I^1 f)$ has been shown in \cite{sharafutdinov_book94, mishra}, while stability estimates and inversions are proposed in \cite{venkeetal19, venkeMishraMonard19}, and most recently in \cite{kunyanskyetal23}. The latter work also includes a numerical implementation.

%; e.g. see \cite{schuster08} 
%for Euclidean domain and 
% \cite{sharafutdinov_book94, paternainSaloUhlmann14} for non-Euclidean domain.
% and references therein. 
%The problem is originally motivated by engineering applications \cite{WHSWW77,norton88,braunHauk,sparSLP95}.

However, if both $I^0f$ and $I^1f$ are given on the manifold of all lines, then the entire vector field is uniquely and stably determined as shown for arbitrary order tensors in
\cite{sharafutdinov_book94,  sharafutdinov17, denisiuk}, with inversion formulas in \cite{derevtsovSvetov15,mishra,derevtsovetal21, venkySharafutdinov_etal2024}, and reconstruction methods proposed in  \cite{kunyanskyetal23, fujiwaraOmogbheSadiqTamasan23,derevtsov23}. 
%Significant progress in the inversion of the momenta ray transform of symmetric tensors in the non-Euclidean setting (where the integration is on geodesics) has also been recently made  \cite{ abhishekMishra}. 
%\commentK{Include non-Euclidean references - Sahoo and some.}

%In the non-Euclidean setting
%the unique determination result was shown for simple real analytic Riemannian manifolds in \cite{abhishekMishra}, extended to simple Riemannian surface in \cite{venkeMishraMonard19}, 
%%also in the attenuated  case in \cite{holmanStefanov},
%with inversion for $m=1$ and sources on a curve in \cite{mishra}, and stability estimates in \cite{sharafutdinov17,venkeetal19}.

For even dimensional domains the problem is non-local, where the determination of $f$ from partial knowledge of  $(I^0f,I^1f)$ on a strict sub-manifold of lines becomes a question of unique analytic continuation \cite{illmavirta}.
Reconstruction methods in problems where uniqueness is based on a unique analytic continuation argument are known to be  exponentially ill-posed \cite{lavrentiev}.

% Consequently the reconstruction of $f$ on its entire support is a severely ill-posed problem.
%\commentK{Refer Lavarentiev book.}
%Consider the subset of lines intersecting an arc $\Lambda$, namely $L_{x,\theta} : = \left \{x+t \theta: t \in \BR, (x,\theta) \in \Sigma_{\pm}  \right\} $ with 
%\[
%\Sigma_{\pm} := \set{ (\zeta, \theta) \in \Lambda\times S^1 \sep \pm \nu(\zeta)\cdot\theta > 0},
%\]
%where $\nu$ is the outer unit normal field at the boundary and $\theta$ is a direction in the unit sphere $S^1$. 

%In this work we introduce a reconstruction method and a numerical algorithm, which quantitatively 

%In here, we recover the vector field  $f$ in $\OM^+$  from the knowledge of $(I^0 f, I^1 f)$ on $\Sigma_{\pm}$.
%We know of no other quantitative imaging method to  recover $f$ (even in part) from partial data.

Let $\OM \subset \BR^2$ be a convex domain containing the support of $f$ and  $\Lambda$ be a smooth arc of its boundary.   %extended by zero outside. 

In here we consider a partial reconstruction problem in which $f$ is to be determined on the convex hull $\OM^+=\co(\Lambda)$  from knowledge of  $I^0f,I^1f$ on the set $\mathcal{S}$ of lines intersecting the arc $\Lambda$. More precisely, $x$ in \eqref{eq:mtrans}  is restricted to $ \Lambda$.
%This work introduces a method which determines $f$ on the convex hull $\OM^+=\co(\Lambda)$ from  $I^0f,I^1f$ restricted to the set $\mathcal{S}$ of lines intersecting the arc $\Lambda$, i.e., $x$ in \eqref{eq:mtrans}  is restricted to $ \Lambda$. 
The reconstruction of $f$ on the subdomain $\Omega^+$ becomes a polynomially ill-posed problem. The proposed algorithm preserves this mildly ill-posedness by virtue of the choice of discretization, and thus achieves numerically stable reconstruction. We know of no other quantitative imaging method to recover $f$ on $\Omega^+$  from this partial data.

