\section{Partial inversion of the momenta Doppler transform} \label{partialInv}

The reconstruction problem is formulated as an inverse boundary value problem for a coupled system of transport equations as in \cite{fujiwaraOmogbheSadiqTamasan23}.
%The novelty here is that the data $I^0f, I^1f$ is known only on the subset of lines $\mathcal{S}$. 
%The reconstruction method below combines two ideas:  Firstly, the problem is recast as an inverse boundary value problem for a system of Bukhgeim-Beltrami equations  \eqref{Beltrami_uk_sys} with partial data on $\Lambda$. Secondly, 
We will show that the trace on $\Lambda$ of the solution of this system uniquely determines the  trace on the inner chord $L$ (inaccessible by direct measurement), thus reducing the problem to an inverse boundary value problem with data on the entire boundary of $\OM^+=\co(\Lambda)$.


Upon a rotation and translation of the domain, we may assume that the chord $L$ joining the endpoints of $\Lambda$ is the interval $(-s,s)$ on the real axis for some $s>0$, and that $\Lambda$ lies in the upper half plane; see Figure \ref{fig:1} for the geometric setting.



For $k =0,1$, let $v^k(x,\theta)$ be the unique solution  
%If $v^k(x,\theta)$, for  $k =0,1$ is the unique solution 
to the system of transport equations:
\begin{subequations}\label{bvp_UK_transport}
	\begin{align}\label{TransportEq_u0}
		\theta\cdot\nabla v^0(x,\theta)  &=  \theta \cdot f(x), 
		\\  \label{TransportEq_uk}
		\theta\cdot\nabla v^1(x,\theta) &=  v^{0}(x,\theta), \quad \text{for } \, (x, \theta) \in  \OM \times S^1,
	\end{align}
	subject to  no incoming fluxes %traces %$u^k \lvert_{\Gam_{-}} =0,$ for $0 \leq k \leq m.$
\begin{align}\label{uk_Gam-}
	v^k(x,\theta) &= 0, \quad \text{on $\Sigma_{-}$},
\end{align}	 
\end{subequations} 
where $\ds \Sigma_{-} := \set{ (\zeta, \theta) \in \del \OM \times S^1 \sep  \nu(\zeta)\cdot\theta < 0}$
with $\nu$ being the outer unit normal field at the boundary.
% and $\theta$ is a direction in the unit sphere $S^1$. 

The outgoing fluxes  are given only on
$$\ds \Sigma_{+} := \set{ (\zeta, \theta) \in \Lambda \times S^1 \sep  \nu(\zeta)\cdot\theta > 0}.$$


The traces on $\Sigma_{+}$ of the solution of the 
boundary value problem \eqref{bvp_UK_transport}  
 are in a one-to-one correspondence with the momenta Doppler transform
%\begin{align}\label{data_equiv1}
